  - `--trust-project` is in `KNOWN_FLAGS`, takes no value (so it is deliberately *not* in the value-taking list), and its state lives beside `set_continue_on_silence`/`is_continue_on_silence` in this file. **Deliberately not covered yet** (follow-up #749): no persisted per-directory trust decision, no interactive prompt, and `[permissions]` / `allow` / `dir_restrictions` from a project-local config are still applied unguarded — only process-starting MCP entries are gated.
  + `--trust-project` is in `KNOWN_FLAGS`, takes no value (so it is deliberately *not* in the value-taking list), and its state lives beside `set_continue_on_silence`/`is_continue_on_silence` in this file. Day 166 (#749 item 3) extended the same boundary to the **privilege-granting half of `[permissions]`**, which was the more urgent gap: a stranger's repo shipping `allow = ["curl *"]` silently auto-approved those bash commands for the whole session, and unlike an MCP entry the effect is invisible until a command runs without asking. The pure `gate_project_permissions(permissions, from_cli_flags, project_local, trusted) -> PermissionGateOutcome` moves `permissions.allow` into `refused_allow` **only** when the config is project-local, `--trust-project` was not passed, and the permissions did not come from CLI flags (a `--allow` the user typed is the user's own word); every other input is a byte-identical pass-through. The gate is **deliberately asymmetric, and that asymmetry is the point**: fields are sorted by which direction they move privilege, and only the granting one is refused — `permissions.deny` is kept verbatim, and `dir_restrictions.allow`/`deny` are not touched at all, because the file-tool default is *unrestricted* so an allow-list can only narrow. A naive mirror of `gate_mcp_sources` would leave yoyo **less** confined than the repo asked for: a regression dressed as a security fix. A repo may always restrict yoyo further; it may not loosen it. Refusal is announced by the pure `project_permission_refusal_message` (names every refused pattern verbatim, both escape hatches, and states that deny/dir restrictions are still in force; silent under `is_quiet()`, glyph-free under `is_plain_output()`). **Both** entry points are wired, because a per-token pass is not a per-entry-point pass: `parse_permission_and_dir_config` in this file, and the `"permissions"` subcommand arm in `dispatch_sub.rs`, which parses the raw config independently to display effective permissions — a display showing refused patterns as active would be a lie in the one place a user goes to check (that arm also reads `--trust-project` straight off `args`, since `try_dispatch_subcommand` runs before `set_trust_project`). **Deliberately not covered yet** (follow-up #749 items 1 and 2): no persisted per-directory trust decision and no interactive prompt — `--trust-project` applies to one run and must be re-passed.
